\begin_body
\begin_layout Title
The centered Magnus exponent and Magnus quadrature formulas of orders 2, 4, 6 and 8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Kenneth C. Johnson
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

ken@kjinnovation.com
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
Version 02-Nov-2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Abstract
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The Magnus exponent is formulated over an integration interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[-t,t]$
\end_inset
 that is centered on the origin, so that the solution at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 is obtained from the solution at 
\begin_inset Formula $-t$
\end_inset
. In this centered formulation the propagator satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $U(-t)=U(t)^{-1}$
\end_inset
, the Magnus exponent is an odd function of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, and the Magnus theorem takes a form in which the even and odd parts of the coefficient matrix appear separately. The Magnus exponent is then expressed in Legendre moments of the coefficient matrix over the centered interval, and quadrature formulas with residual errors of order 
\begin_inset Formula $t^3$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $t^5$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $t^7$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t^9$
\end_inset
 are given, using 0, 1, 3 and 6 commutators with the moments evaluated by Gauss-Legendre, Newton-Cotes or Gauss-Lobatto quadrature, and Gauss-Kronrod rules are shown to give embedded error estimates. The methodology and results are compared with those of Blanes et al. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2000"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Linear differential equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
first-order, linear differential equation
\emph default
 or system of differential equations has the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F'(t)=A(t)\cdot F(t)+B(t)\label{eq:ode}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
where 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t)$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B(t)$
\end_inset
 are vector functions and 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)$
\end_inset
 is a square-matrix function of scalar argument 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
. The equation is 
\emph on
autonomous
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
 are constant, and is 
\emph on
homogeneous
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $B(t)$
\end_inset
 is identically zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the centered formulation, the integration interval is 
\begin_inset Formula $[-t,t]$
\end_inset
: the value 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-t)$
\end_inset
 is specified and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t)$
\end_inset
 is to be determined. Any interval can be put in this form by shifting the time origin to its midpoint; an interval of length 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset
 centered at 
\begin_inset Formula $t_0$
\end_inset
 corresponds to 
\begin_inset Formula $t=h/2$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $A(\tau)$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\tau)$
\end_inset
 replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t_0+\tau)$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B(t_0+\tau)$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A nonhomogeneous equation can be converted to a homogeneous equation by using an alternative formulation of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ode"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt}\begin{pmatrix}F(t)\\ 1\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}A(t) & B(t)\\ \mathbf{0} & 0\end{pmatrix}\cdot\begin{pmatrix}F(t)\\ 1\end{pmatrix}\label{eq:aug}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset
 represents a zero row vector. Therefore, we need only consider homogeneous equations, although Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:aug"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 might be implemented to efficiently take advantage of the zeros in the coefficient matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With 
\begin_inset Formula $B(t)=\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset
, the value 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t)$
\end_inset
 is a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-t)$
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F(t)=U(t)\cdot F(-t)\label{eq:centered}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
where the propagator 
\begin_inset Formula $U(t)$
\end_inset
 is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U'(t)=A(t)\cdot U(t)+U(t)\cdot A(-t),\quad U(0)=\mathbf{I}\label{eq:uode}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{I}$
\end_inset
 is the identity matrix. Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uode"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 follows from differentiating Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:centered"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, using 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d}{dt}F(-t)=-F'(-t)=-A(-t)\cdot F(-t)$
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F'(t)=U'(t)\cdot F(-t)-U(t)\cdot A(-t)\cdot F(-t)\label{eq:diffcent}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
and equating this with 
\begin_inset Formula $F'(t)=A(t)\cdot F(t)=A(t)\cdot U(t)\cdot F(-t)$
\end_inset
. Both expressions hold for every solution 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset
, and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-t)$
\end_inset
 can take any value, so the matrix coefficients of 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-t)$
\end_inset
 are equal; this is Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uode"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
. The initial condition 
\begin_inset Formula $U(0)=\mathbf{I}$
\end_inset
 is Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:centered"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $U(t)$
\end_inset
 is related to the conventional (uncentered) propagator 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
, defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V'(t)=A(t)\cdot V(t),\quad V(0)=\mathbf{I}\label{eq:vode}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
which gives 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t)=V(t)\cdot F(0)$
\end_inset
 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
. Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t)=V(t)\cdot F(0)$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-t)=V(-t)\cdot F(0)$
\end_inset
, and eliminating 
\begin_inset Formula $F(0)$
\end_inset
 gives Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:centered"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 with
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(t)=V(t)\cdot V(-t)^{-1}\label{eq:uv}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $-t$
\end_inset
 in Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 gives the time-reversal property of the centered propagator,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(-t)=V(-t)\cdot V(t)^{-1}=U(t)^{-1}\label{eq:uinv}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
(Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uinv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 also follows directly from Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:centered"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
: 
\begin_inset Formula $F(-t)=U(-t)\cdot F(t)$
\end_inset
.) The centered propagator and the time-reversal property of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uinv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 appear in Blanes et al. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcor2009"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 (its Eqs. 63-66), where they are used to show that the Magnus exponent of a step contains only odd powers of the step size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The centered Magnus exponent
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uode"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 has an exponential-matrix solution,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(t)=\exp(\Omega(t))\label{eq:uexp}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
If the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 at all times commute with each other, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 is the integral of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 over the interval, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)=\int_{-t}^{t}dt_1\cdot A(t_1)$
\end_inset
, but we are concerned here with the case where this condition does not hold. In general, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 (the 
\emph on
centered Magnus exponent
\emph default
) is defined by the conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt}\exp(\Omega(t))=A(t)\cdot\exp(\Omega(t))+\exp(\Omega(t))\cdot A(-t),\quad\Omega(0)=\mathbf{0}\label{eq:omegadef}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
For 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 small enough that the matrix logarithm near 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{I}$
\end_inset
 is unique, Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uinv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset
 is an odd function of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega(-t)=-\Omega(t)\label{eq:odd}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Commutator algebra
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The derivations below use the following definitions and relations. The 
\emph on
commutator
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $[\ldots]$
\end_inset
 is defined, for square matrix arguments 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
, as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
[A,B]=A\cdot B-B\cdot A\label{eq:comm}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
The commutator is antisymmetric and satisfies the Jacobi identity,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
[B,A]=-[A,B]\label{eq:anti}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
[A,[B,C]]+[B,[C,A]]+[C,[A,B]]=\mathbf{0}\label{eq:jacobi}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
The 
\emph on
adjoint mapping
\emph default
 “
\begin_inset Formula $ad$
\end_inset
” is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)=\underbrace{[\Omega,[\Omega,\ldots[\Omega}_{j\text{ times}},X]]]:\quad ad_{\Omega}^{0}(X)=X,\quad ad_{\Omega}^{j+1}(X)=[\Omega,ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)]\label{eq:addef}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

\begin_inset Formula $ad$
\end_inset
 has the following properties,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
ad_{\Omega}^{j}(a\cdot X+b\cdot Y)=a\cdot ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)+b\cdot ad_{\Omega}^{j}(Y)\quad(\text{for scalars }a\text{ and }b)\label{eq:adlin}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
ad_{\sigma\cdot\Omega}^{j}(X)=\sigma^{j}\cdot ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)\quad(\text{scalar }\sigma)\label{eq:adscale}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
ad_{\Omega}^{j+k}(X)=ad_{\Omega}^{j}(ad_{\Omega}^{k}(X))\label{eq:adcomp}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Two exponential identities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The similarity transformation 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\Omega)\cdot X\cdot\exp(-\Omega)$
\end_inset
 has a series expansion of the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\exp(\Omega)\cdot X\cdot\exp(-\Omega)=\sum_{j}\frac{1}{j!}\cdot ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)\label{eq:sim}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
This relation is derived as follows: For scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset
, the following relation is obtained by induction,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{d^{j}}{d\sigma^{j}}\big(\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega)\cdot X\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega)\big)=\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega)\cdot ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega)\label{eq:simind}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Hence, the expression 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega)\cdot X\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega)$
\end_inset
 has the Taylor series expansion
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega)\cdot X\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega)=\sum_{j}\frac{\sigma^{j}}{j!}\cdot ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)\label{eq:simtaylor}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sim"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 follows from Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:simtaylor"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\Omega(t))$
\end_inset
 has the following relationship to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega'(t)$
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt}\exp(\Omega(t))=\left(\sum_{j}\frac{1}{(j+1)!}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{j}(\Omega'(t))\right)\cdot\exp(\Omega(t))\label{eq:dexp}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
This relation is derived by first applying Eqs. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sim"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:adscale"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 to simplify the following expression,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{\partial}{\partial\sigma}\left(\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\right)\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\right)\\
&=\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\big(\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\cdot\Omega(t)\big)\right)\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\\
&\quad-\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\right)\cdot\Omega(t)\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\\
&=\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\cdot\Omega'(t)\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\\
&=\sum_{j}\frac{1}{j!}\cdot ad_{\sigma\cdot\Omega(t)}^{j}(\Omega'(t))=\sum_{j}\frac{\sigma^{j}}{j!}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{j}(\Omega'(t))
\end{aligned}\label{eq:dexpsigma}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:dexpsigma"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is integrated over 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset
 to obtain Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:dexp"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\exp(\Omega(t))\right)\cdot\exp(-\Omega(t))\\
&=\int_{0}^{1}d\sigma\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial\sigma}\left(\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\exp(\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\right)\cdot\exp(-\sigma\cdot\Omega(t))\right)\\
&=\sum_{j}\frac{1}{(j+1)!}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{j}(\Omega'(t))
\end{aligned}\label{eq:dexpint}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The centered Magnus theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last term in Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:omegadef"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is rewritten with Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sim"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\Omega(t))\cdot A(-t)=\big(\exp(\Omega(t))\cdot A(-t)\cdot\exp(-\Omega(t))\big)\cdot\exp(\Omega(t))=\big(\sum_{j}\frac{1}{j!}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{j}(A(-t))\big)\cdot\exp(\Omega(t))$
\end_inset
. Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:dexp"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 and this expression are substituted into Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:omegadef"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, and the common right factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\Omega(t))$
\end_inset
 is cancelled,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{j}\frac{1}{(j+1)!}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{j}(\Omega'(t))=A(t)+\sum_{j}\frac{1}{j!}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{j}(A(-t))\label{eq:subst}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
This relation is inverted to solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega'(t)$
\end_inset
,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
The centered Magnus theorem
\emph default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Omega'(t)&=\sum_{k}\frac{B_{k}}{k!}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{k}\big(A(t)+(-1)^{k}\cdot A(-t)\big)\\
&=A(t)+A(-t)-\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot[\Omega(t),A(t)-A(-t)]+\tfrac{1}{12}\cdot[\Omega(t),[\Omega(t),A(t)+A(-t)]]\\
&\quad-\tfrac{1}{720}\cdot[\Omega(t),[\Omega(t),[\Omega(t),[\Omega(t),A(t)+A(-t)]]]]+O(t^{6})\\
&\quad\big(\text{with }\Omega(0)=\mathbf{0},\ \Omega(t)=O(t)\big)
\end{aligned}\label{eq:theorem}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
The 
\begin_inset Formula $B_k$
\end_inset
 factors are the 
\emph on
Bernoulli numbers
\emph default
, defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&B_{0}=1;\quad\text{For }j>0,\ B_{j}=-\sum_{k=0}^{j-1}\frac{j!}{(j-k+1)!\cdot k!}\cdot B_{k}\\
&\{B_{0},B_{1},\ldots\}=\{1,-\tfrac{1}{2},\tfrac{1}{6},0,-\tfrac{1}{30},0,\tfrac{1}{42},0,-\tfrac{1}{30},0,\tfrac{5}{66},0,-\tfrac{691}{2730},\ldots\}
\end{aligned}\label{eq:bernoulli}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
(The truncated series residual in Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:theorem"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^6)$
\end_inset
, not 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^5)$
\end_inset
, because 
\begin_inset Formula $B_5=0$
\end_inset
.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:theorem"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is obtained from Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:subst"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 as follows. For a power series 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)=\sum_j f_j\cdot x^j$
\end_inset
 with scalar coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $f_j$
\end_inset
, define the operator
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(ad_{\Omega})(X)=\sum_{j}f_{j}\cdot ad_{\Omega}^{j}(X)\label{eq:opdef}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
By Eqs. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:adlin"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:adcomp"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, applying two such operators in succession is the same as applying the operator of the product series,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(ad_{\Omega})\big(g(ad_{\Omega})(X)\big)=(f\cdot g)(ad_{\Omega})(X)\label{eq:opprod}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
where 
\begin_inset Formula $(f\cdot g)(x)=f(x)\cdot g(x)=\sum_j\big(\sum_{k=0}^{j}f_k\cdot g_{j-k}\big)\cdot x^j$
\end_inset
. Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:subst"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 has the form 
\begin_inset Formula $s(ad_{\Omega(t)})(\Omega'(t))=A(t)+e(ad_{\Omega(t)})(A(-t))$
\end_inset
, with the power series
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s(x)=\sum_{j}\frac{x^{j}}{(j+1)!},\quad e(x)=\sum_{j}\frac{x^{j}}{j!},\quad\beta(x)=\sum_{k}\frac{B_{k}}{k!}\cdot x^{k}\label{eq:series}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
(
\begin_inset Formula $e(x)$
\end_inset
 is the exponential series, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\cdot s(x)=e(x)-1$
\end_inset
.) The coefficient of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^j$
\end_inset
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(x)\cdot s(x)$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{j}\frac{B_k}{k!\cdot(j-k+1)!}$
\end_inset
, which by Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bernoulli"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is 1 for 
\begin_inset Formula $j=0$
\end_inset
 and 0 for 
\begin_inset Formula $j>0$
\end_inset
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\beta(x)\cdot s(x)=1\label{eq:betas}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Multiplying Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:betas"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset
 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(x)\cdot(e(x)-1)=x$
\end_inset
. Replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $-x$
\end_inset
 and multiplying by 
\begin_inset Formula $-e(x)$
\end_inset
 (using 
\begin_inset Formula $e(-x)\cdot e(x)=1$
\end_inset
) gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(-x)\cdot(e(x)-1)=x\cdot e(x)=\beta(x)\cdot e(x)\cdot(e(x)-1)$
\end_inset
. The common factor 
\begin_inset Formula $e(x)-1=x\cdot s(x)$
\end_inset
 can be cancelled (power series have no zero divisors), so
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\beta(x)\cdot e(x)=\beta(-x)\label{eq:betae}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Now 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(ad_{\Omega(t)})$
\end_inset
 is applied to both sides of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:subst"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
. By Eqs. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:opprod"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:betas"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, the left side becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega'(t)$
\end_inset
. By Eqs. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:opprod"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:betae"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, the right side becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(ad_{\Omega(t)})(A(t))+\beta(-ad_{\Omega(t)})(A(-t))$
\end_inset
, which is the first line of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:theorem"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:betae"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 also determines the odd Bernoulli numbers. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(x)\cdot(e(x)-1)=x$
\end_inset
, it gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(-x)=\beta(x)+x$
\end_inset
; the odd terms of this power-series identity are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B_{1}=-\tfrac{1}{2},\quad B_{k}=0\ \ (k\text{ odd},\ k\ge3)\label{eq:oddbern}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Hence the centered Magnus theorem separates into the even part 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)+A(-t)$
\end_inset
 and the odd part 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)-A(-t)$
\end_inset
 of the coefficient matrix,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega'(t)=\sum_{k\text{ even}}\frac{B_{k}}{k!}\cdot ad_{\Omega(t)}^{k}\big(A(t)+A(-t)\big)-\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot\big[\Omega(t),A(t)-A(-t)\big]\label{eq:evenodd}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Time-reversal symmetry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is consistent with the odd symmetry of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:odd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
. If 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 is an odd function of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, then each term on the right side of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is an even function of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
: 
\begin_inset Formula $ad_{\Omega(t)}^k$
\end_inset
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 even preserves the even parity of 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)+A(-t)$
\end_inset
, and one commutator with the odd 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 turns the odd 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)-A(-t)$
\end_inset
 into an even function. So 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega'(t)$
\end_inset
 is even, and its integral with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(0)=\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset
 is odd. When 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 is expanded in a Taylor series about 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset
 and Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 is solved by successive approximation, every term of the resulting series for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 is therefore an odd power of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The leading term is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)=\int_{-t}^{t}dt_1\cdot A(t_1)+O(t^3)$
\end_inset
, from 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega'(t)=A(t)+A(-t)+O(t^2)$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Centered Legendre moments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The quadrature formulas below express 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 in terms of Legendre moments of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 over the centered interval,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g_{k}(t)=\int_{-t}^{t}P_{k}(\tau/t)\cdot A(\tau)\,d\tau\label{eq:gdef}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
where 
\begin_inset Formula $P_k$
\end_inset
 is the Legendre polynomial of degree 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
, defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{0}(u)=1,\quad P_{1}(u)=u,\quad(k+1)\cdot P_{k+1}(u)=(2k+1)\cdot u\cdot P_{k}(u)-k\cdot P_{k-1}(u)\label{eq:pdef}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
The polynomials needed for Magnus formulas of orders up to 10 are listed in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:legendre"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
; a formula of order 
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
 uses 
\begin_inset Formula $P_0,\ldots,P_{n-1}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

