\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The Legendre polynomials are orthogonal on 
\begin_inset Formula $[-1,1]$
\end_inset
 and have definite parity,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int_{-1}^{1}P_{j}(u)\cdot P_{k}(u)\,du=\frac{2}{2k+1}\cdot\delta_{jk},\quad P_{k}(-u)=(-1)^{k}\cdot P_{k}(u)\label{eq:porth}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
(
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{jk}$
\end_inset
 is the Kronecker delta.) 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0(t)$
\end_inset
 is the integral of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 over the interval, and if 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 is constant then 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k(t)=\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\ge1$
\end_inset
. With 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 expanded in a Taylor series about 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=0$
\end_inset
, Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gdef"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g_{k}(t)=\sum_{j\ge k,\ j-k\text{ even}}c_{kj}\cdot\frac{t^{j+1}}{j!}\cdot A^{(j)}(0),\quad c_{kj}=\int_{-1}^{1}P_{k}(u)\cdot u^{j}\,du\label{eq:gtaylor}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
(
\begin_inset Formula $c_{kj}=0$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $j<k$
\end_inset
 and for odd 
\begin_inset Formula $j-k$
\end_inset
), so 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k(t)=O(t^{k+1})$
\end_inset
, with leading coefficient 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{kk}=2^{k+1}\cdot(k!)^2/(2k+1)!$
\end_inset
. We call 
\begin_inset Formula $k+1$
\end_inset
 the 
\emph on
weight
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
; the weight of a product or commutator of moments is the sum of the weights of its factors, and a term of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^n)$
\end_inset
. Replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $-t$
\end_inset
 in Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gdef"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\to-\tau$
\end_inset
 and using Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:porth"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 shows that the moments have definite parity,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g_{k}(-t)=(-1)^{k+1}\cdot g_{k}(t)\label{eq:gpar}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_2$
\end_inset
 are odd functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_1$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_3$
\end_inset
 are even, and a product of moments of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 has parity 
\begin_inset Formula $(-1)^n$
\end_inset
. Conversely, by orthogonality the moments determine 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 through its Legendre series,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A(\tau)=\sum_{k}\frac{2k+1}{2t}\cdot g_{k}(t)\cdot P_{k}(\tau/t),\quad-t\le\tau\le t\label{eq:alegendre}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
which is a finite sum when 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 is a polynomial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The Magnus exponent in Legendre moments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Taylor-series solution of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 can be re-expressed in the moments by inverting Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gtaylor"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
. This was done in exact rational arithmetic, by successive approximation of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 through order 
\begin_inset Formula $t^9$
\end_inset
. Through weight 7 the result is, in the Lyndon basis of nested commutators,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Omega(t)={}&g_{0}\\
&-\tfrac{1}{2}[g_{0},g_{1}]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{2}[g_{1},g_{2}]+\tfrac{1}{12}[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{2}]]\\
&+\tfrac{3}{20}[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}]+\tfrac{1}{120}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{2}[g_{2},g_{3}]+\tfrac{1}{10}[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{3}]]\\
&+\tfrac{5}{84}[[g_{0},g_{2}],g_{2}]-\tfrac{1}{20}[[g_{0},g_{3}],g_{1}]\\
&-\tfrac{3}{14}[g_{1},[g_{1},g_{2}]]-\tfrac{1}{120}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{3}]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{168}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{2}]]]-\tfrac{1}{28}[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{2}],g_{1}]]\\
&-\tfrac{3}{56}[[g_{0},g_{1}],[g_{0},g_{2}]]-\tfrac{9}{280}[[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}],g_{1}]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{504}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{2}]]]]-\tfrac{1}{280}[g_{0},[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}]]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{210}[[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]],[g_{0},g_{1}]]-\tfrac{1}{5040}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]]]]]\\
&+O(t^{9})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:target}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
Three properties of this series are used below. (i) Only odd weights occur, as Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:odd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 requires. (ii) The only term outside commutators is 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
. (iii) Through weight 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-1$
\end_inset
, only 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_{n-1}$
\end_inset
 occur; 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
 first appears at weight 
\begin_inset Formula $2k+1$
\end_inset
. A formula of order 
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
, with residual 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^{2n+1})$
\end_inset
, therefore needs only the moments 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_{n-1}$
\end_inset
, and it must reproduce the coefficients of the series at odd weights up to 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-1$
\end_inset
 (the 
\emph on
order conditions
\emph default
, Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:conditions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
):
\end_layout

