\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The last column is the fewest commutators found, from a rank survey and a numerical search over commutator patterns; Blanes, Casas and Ros 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bcr2002"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 state the same minimum for orders 4, 6 and 8. The series in Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:target"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 agrees term by term with the expansion of the conventional Magnus exponent about the midpoint of a step of length 
\begin_inset Formula $2t$
\end_inset
, which provides an independent check of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:evenodd"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The formulas below are nested commutators 
\begin_inset Formula $C_i=[X_i,Y_i]$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_i$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_i$
\end_inset
 are linear combinations of moments and earlier commutators, and each operand has definite parity. A commutator of two odd or two even operands is even and is used only as an operand; the commutators that enter 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 are odd. Each formula is therefore odd in 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, like 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
, and only its odd-weight terms need to be matched.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Moment quadrature
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The moments are computed by quadrature from samples 
\begin_inset Formula $A(u_i\cdot t)$
\end_inset
 at nodes 
\begin_inset Formula $u_i\in[-1,1]$
\end_inset
. For a formula of order 
\begin_inset Formula $2n$
\end_inset
 it suffices that the quadrature integrate polynomials of degree 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-1$
\end_inset
 exactly. A rule of polynomial degree 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset
 computes 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
 with an error made of multiples of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_m$
\end_inset
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $m\ge d+1-k$
\end_inset
, which raises the weight by at least 
\begin_inset Formula $d+1-2k$
\end_inset
. Because 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
 occurs only in terms of weight at least 
\begin_inset Formula $2k+1$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $d=2n-1$
\end_inset
 the moment errors contribute only terms of weight 
\begin_inset Formula $2n+1$
\end_inset
 or higher. In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
, which occurs alone, is accurate to 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^{2n+1})$
\end_inset
, while the other moments, which occur only inside commutators with a partner of order 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, need only be accurate to 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^{2n})$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Gauss-Legendre quadrature.
\emph default
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
