\begin_layout Standard
The entries marked (new) in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:cost"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 count new evaluations of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
. In a multi-step integration each step reuses the sample at the boundary it shares with the previous step, so the number of new evaluations per step is one less than the number of quadrature nodes. With this reuse the Gauss-Lobatto rule needs no more evaluations than the Gauss-Legendre rule. A Gauss-Kronrod pair needs 
\begin_inset Formula $2n+1$
\end_inset
 new evaluations per step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In adaptive integration, where a step is split into two half-steps when its error estimate is too large, the Newton-Cotes rule has a further advantage. The nodes of the two half-steps include all the nodes of the original step, so half of the samples they need are already available, and the number of new evaluations is reduced by a factor of two: 
\begin_inset Formula $2n-2$
\end_inset
 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $4n-4$
\end_inset
 for order 
\begin_inset Formula $2n\ge4$
\end_inset
. The Gauss-Legendre nodes are not reused after splitting, and the Gauss-Lobatto rule reuses only the endpoints and, with five nodes, the midpoint; splitting a step then costs 5 and 6 new evaluations at orders 6 and 8 with Gauss-Lobatto nodes, compared with 4 and 6 with Newton-Cotes nodes and 6 and 8 with Gauss-Legendre nodes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The formulas were tested on three 
\begin_inset Formula $4\times4$
\end_inset
 matrix polynomials 
\begin_inset Formula $A(\tau)=\sum_{k=0}^{12}C_k\cdot\tau^k$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $C_k=s\cdot d^k\cdot N_k$
\end_inset
, where the 
\begin_inset Formula $N_k$
\end_inset
 are standard normal random matrices (NumPy default_rng with the stated seed), and parameters (seed, 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset
) of 
\begin_inset Formula $(42,1,0.8)$
\end_inset
 (random), 
\begin_inset Formula $(9,2,0.5)$
\end_inset
 (commutator-heavy) and 
\begin_inset Formula $(7,0.3,1.5)$
\end_inset
 (rapidly varying relative to its non-commutativity). The reference is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)=\log(V(t)\cdot V(-t)^{-1})$
\end_inset
, Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:uv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
